A1. This helps an Englishman to get along. ~ TRAM
A5. A help wanted signal. ~ SOS
A8. The word naming this fish is two-thirds of the word naming its reflection. ~ SHAD
A12. An abbreviation for a word which is the preoccupation of certain high-minded young men. ~ AERO
A13. The desire for this has led to a lot of monkeygland business. ~ PEP
A14. This is one step. ~ PACE
A15. This should be driven home. ~ NAIL
A16. This is either a macaw or a constellation. (Take your choice.) ~ ARA
A17. This girl's name starts and ends with an article. ~ ANNA
A18. This is rough on the surface. ~ GRATER
A20. A word the wise will heed. ~ DANGER
A22. This is too fast in a taxicab. ~ METER
A24. These shine in the theatre—down front. ~ PATES
A28. This will stay with you if you're tight. ~ MONEY
A33. It takes brains to get this. ~ IDEA
A34. Shakespeare made much of this—about nothing. ~ ADO
A35. How many bootleggers favor repeal of the 18th Amendment? ~ NONE
A36. It's very sad to drop these. ~ TEARS
A38. What one does who allots, portions out. ~ METES
A39. An item that looks well on the balance sheet. ~ ASSET
A42. Bars often make this impossible. ~ ESCAPE
A46. To insist upon one's rights. ~ ASSERT
A51. This will certainly age a person. ~ YEAR
A52. What many do to get the dirt. ~ DIG
A54. This is an Indian tribe ~ CREE
A55. This is ever green. ~ ERIN
A56. What Adam did to get his walking-papers. ~ ATE
A57. These are helpful to military men. ~ AIDS
A58. A very famous Western city. ~ RENO
A59. A conjunction. ~ NOR
A60. What most people lie to do. ~ REST

D1. Strong taste or flavor. ~ TANG
D2. What we'd prefer to bring up in a military Invasion. ~ REAR
D3. This has an air about it. ~ ARIA
D4. To shed. ~ MOLT
D5. What you need to retire. ~ SPARE
D6. This is over for a poet. ~ OER
D7. This gets many a laborer into a hole. ~ SPADE
D8. To extend across. ~ SPAN
D9. What the old desperado did when he got to the end of his rope. ~ HANG
D10. A skin disease. ~ ACNE
D11. A term of endearment. ~ DEAR
D19. Printer's measures. ~ EMS
D21. What the boys like best about the girls. ~ ARM
D23. These are always rising and falling. ~ TIDES
D24. The heart of a peach. ~ PIT
D25. This is soft stuff. (Suffix.) ~ ADE
D26. You get this with your 5 o'clock girl. ~ TEA
D27. This picks up scandal. ~ EAR
D29. This is singular. ~ ONE
D30. This is negative. ~ NOT
D31. This is about two o'clock on the compass. ~ ENE
D32. An easy word to say in a speakeasy. ~ YES
D37. This is vital stuff. ~ SAP
D38. These are the high places. (Abbr.) ~ MTS
D40. This travels around. ~ SEDAN
D41. Full of keen de ire. ~ EAGER
D42. What a corner lounger might be called. ~ EYER
D43. Withered; dried up. ~ SERE
D44. A famous homicide. ~ CAIN
D45. A river of Tuscany. ~ ARNO
D47. A sign of an old wound. ~ SCAR
D48. A lake. ~ ERIE
D49. The hobgoblin of some 100% Americans. ~ REDS
D50. This should be applied to prohibition liquor. ~ TEST
D53. A foreign statesman made famous by crossword puzzles. ~ ITO


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
A15: This should be driven home. → NAIL (answer key prints HAIL; 1 Down 'Strong taste or flavor' is TANG)
D42: What a corner lounger might be called. → desire

Warnings:
Note: The printed grid gives a number (28) to a square that starts no entry, so from there on the printed clue numbers run one higher than the standard numbering used here: printed 29 Across is 28 Across here, and so on to printed 61 Across (60 Across here); printed 30 Down is 29 Down here, through printed 54 Down (53 Down here). The clues are as printed, each under its own entry. The solution grid that ran on September 8, 1928 printed 1 Down as TAHG. It has been corrected to TANG.

